    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Stomata, gas-exchange valves in the epidermis of plants, provide a useful developmental model for regulation of cell division and differentiation. The TSO/SOL protein family members play key roles in certain division steps and fate transitions within the stomatal lineage, which can drastically affect plant physiological fitness via stomatal shape and patterning. Recent work in the dicot plant Arabidopsis thaliana found that mutants with SOL1 and SOL2 deletions display increased small cells and instances of stomatal clustering, which both indicate extra cell divisions in stomatal precursor cells. However, deletion of SOL2 in the monocot species Brachypodium distachyon leads to incomplete divisions in stomatal guard cells. To determine the functional similarity between these homologous proteins, we cloned the Brachypodium SOL2 gene into Arabidopsis sol1;sol2 double mutants expressed under Arabidopsis SOL, TSO1, and overexpression promoters. The transformed Arabidopsis sol1;sol2 mutants displayed a lack of expression of Brachypodium SOL2 protein despite confirmed presence of the BdSOL2 gene. If the expression of BdSOL2 in Arabidopsis is substantially different from AtSOL1 and AtSOL2 expression, this result could imply a diversification of roles within the TSO/SOL gene family. The specialization of homologous proteins could contribute to the developmental differences of the stomatal lineage between the two species. These findings are a start towards further understanding of the stomatal lineage as a model for species-specific cellular development and of functional roles for TSO/SOL proteins in general cell cycle control.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
